  "releaseNotes": "- Fixed: **a document inserted or updated is ranked right straight away.** After an insert, an\n        update or a field update, a word new to the index, or one that had appeared only in the first\n        searchable field, was scored with the first field's weight and boost until the next `Index()`.\n        A word found only in a low-weight field such as a description could then lift the document as\n        far as a title match would. It now counts with the field it is in.\n      - Fixed: **apostrophes no longer split a word or stand in its way.** \"Children’s\" is now the word\n        \"childrens\" and \"don't\" is \"dont\", for every apostrophe people type (' ’ ‘ ʼ `). Before, the\n        straight ' split the word (\"gardener\" + \"s\") and the typographic ’ stayed inside it, so a search\n        without the apostrophe, or with a typo, missed or ranked low a title such as \"The Gardener’s\n        Daughter\". The default text rules always meant to remove the apostrophe; they ran too late to\n        reach the words.\n      - Changed: **the text rules now apply to whole words and to near-exact matching,** not only to the\n        index's internal fragments. In the default rules ':' and ';' now become a space rather than being\n        removed. If you have your own `TextNormalizer` rule that removes a separator, such as `(\":\", \"\")`,\n        it now joins the words on either side; map it to a space instead.\n      - Changed: **ranking moves for text with apostrophes, colons and semicolons,** as the fixes above\n        take effect. An index picks this up at its next `Index()`, which the server runs whenever it loads\n        a dataset, so there is nothing to migrate. One deliberate trade-off: French or Italian elision such\n        as \"l'homme\" becomes one word, \"lhomme\".",
  "requireLicenseAcceptance": true,
  "title": "IndxSearchLib",
